// The village — the hub between the castle and the dungeon, and the first
// place in the game where nothing is trying to kill you.
//
// It is a *hub*, so what it contains is deliberately not content: a safe
// spawn, somebody to talk to, a shop-shaped building with no shop behind it,
// and a gate east. There are no hostile NPCs at all — the one NPC here is a
// `villager`, which is a `Patrol` with no `Hostile`, so it paces and cannot be
// fought (`Team::Player` also means the player cannot cut it down; see
// `crate::systems::dialogue`).
//
// The elder is Runa, and she is the `villager` kind — the same one
// `testbed_village.ron` places, opening the same graph
// (assets/data/dialogue/elder.ron, id `elder_intro`) because that is the graph
// a villager's `interact:` names in assets/data/stats.ron. This map cannot
// bring its own conversation with it: a graph nothing points at fails
// `every_dialogue_graph_is_reachable_from_something` in tests/data.rs, and the
// only way to point at one is a kind's stat block. So a second conversation is
// a stat block plus a dialogue file, not a map edit — and until there is a
// second talking kind, every villager in the game says what Runa says.
//
// That is also where N-2's story beat belongs. The premise — a fighter taken
// out of a medieval war by people who came for pre-electricity warriors after
// a solar flare took their own machines, in a galaxy where the one thing the
// abductors cannot do is magic, which is why the spells are the progression —
// is a thing `elder_intro` has to say, and its file is owned by whoever is
// writing quest text. This map's job is to put her somewhere you cannot walk
// past, which is what the doorstep below is for.
//
// Legend: '#' solid  'P' spawn.  The villager has no grid character — what
// distinguishes one talking NPC from another is the conversation it carries,
// not where it is stood, so it is an `Npc(kind:, cell:)` entry.
//
// 48x12 tiles at 32px = 1536x384 world pixels. It was 40 wide until the east
// gate: the eight columns added past the old road were added, not moved, so
// everything below measured in columns 0-39 is where it always was.
//
//   ground       rows 10-11, the full width — surface y=320
//   spawn        col 2, row 9    world (70, 286), feet on the ground, and
//                nothing lethal anywhere in the map to spawn near
//   cottage      cols 9-14   roof row 4, jambs rows 5-7 — Runa's
//   doorstep     cols 11-12, a notch cut out of row 10 — floor y=352,
//                one step down out of the street, under the cottage
//   market hall  cols 18-26  roof row 3, posts rows 4-7 at cols 19 and 25
//   counter      cols 21-23, row 9   surface y=288 — the shop, such as it is
//   gatehouse    cols 32-36  roof row 3, mass rows 4-7 at cols 33-35
//   road east    cols 36-45, past the castle gate
//   east gate    cols 44-45, a portcullis locked with `gate_key` — Runa's to
//                give once the Warden is dead, and the way to the Thornwood
//   town wall    cols 46-47, rows 2-9
//
// **Why nothing solid reaches the ground except the counter.** A building that
// crossed the street at head height would close the hub: the player is 34px
// tall and cannot fit under a 32px gap, and the roofs here are far too high to
// jump onto. So every wall stops at row 7 — underside y=256, which leaves the
// 64px opening the gatehouse is read through — and the only thing standing on
// the street is the shop counter, one 32px step that a jump clears.
// `tapes/village_smoke.tape` walks the whole width and proves it.
//
// **Why Runa has a sunken doorstep.** A villager paces until a wall or the
// edge of what it is standing on turns it round, which makes its route as wide
// as the floor it is on — the full 1280px of this street, if she were stood on
// it. The notch is the same trick `testbed_village.ron` uses its bay for, and
// it is load-bearing for the same reason: it pens her into a thirty-pixel beat
// (x 358..388) *and* it stops the player somewhere definite, against the far
// face at x=396. From there her box is inside the 24px interact reach at every
// tick of her beat rather than at some of them, so the tape can say "walk east
// until the step stops you, and she is there" and mean it. Walking past her is
// impossible for the same reason: the street drops out from under you.
Level(
    tileset: "castle",
    name: "Ashford",
    grid: [
        "................................................",
        "................................................",
        "..............................................##",
        "..................#########.....#####.........##",
        ".........######....#.....#.......###..........##",
        ".........#....#....#.....#.......###..........##",
        ".........#....#....#.....#.......###..........##",
        ".........#....#....#.....#.......###..........##",
        "..............................................##",
        "..P..................###......................##",
        "###########..###################################",
        "################################################",
    ],
    entities: [
        // Runa, on her doorstep. The cell is the one whose *floor* she stands
        // on, which for a notch cut out of row 10 is row 10 itself.
        Npc(kind: "villager", cell: (11, 10)),
        // Past the last prompt `tapes/village_smoke.tape` checks, so the walk
        // it pins does not change what it asserts.
        Npc(kind: "pedlar", cell: (31, 9)),
        // The village is the hub: the stair down to the old dungeon — which
        // the crypt's long stair also comes up by — and the castle's gate.
        Door(id: "dungeon_stair", cell: (26, 9), to: "maps/dungeon.ron", at: "village_stair", art: "archway"),
        Door(id: "castle_gate", cell: (37, 9), to: "maps/castle.ron", at: "shaft", art: "door"),
        // The way to the Thornwood, barred since the knights came down. Runa
        // gives the key once the Warden is dead (dialogue/elder.ron); it is
        // kept, so the gate can be walked back through.
        Door(
            id: "east_gate",
            cell: (44, 9),
            to: "maps/thornwood.ron",
            at: "village_gate",
            locked: "gate_key",
            keep_key: true,
            art: "portcullis",
        ),
        // Where the light from the Ark sets the player down at the end, and
        // what Ashford says about it — only once the Choir is dead, so every
        // walk through the street before then is the walk it always was.
        Spawn(id: "sky", cell: (6, 9)),
        Trigger(id: "homecoming", cell: (5, 7), size: (3, 3), dialogue: "epilogue_home", when: "quest.ark.choir"),
    ],
)
